# AI Prompt Log

**Prompt 1:** 
"I want to build a Book Tracker web page using plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Features: add a book with title, author and status, show the list newest first, search, change status, and keep the data after a refresh. Before writing any code, list the files you will create and explain how the data will be saved."

**What happened:** 
The AI explained how it would use `localStorage` for saving the data in the browser and confirmed the files needed.

**Prompt 2:**
"Create index.html, style.css and app.js. Add a form with Title, Author and a Status dropdown (To read, Reading, Finished). When I click Add, show the book at the top of a list. Title and Author are required. If one is empty, show a message under that box."

**What happened:** 
The AI initially tried to bundle everything into a single HTML file using a code canvas tool. It successfully added validation logic to show "Please enter a title" messages when fields were left empty. 

**Prompt 3:** 
"i have to make it go online so keep that in mind"

**What happened:** 
The AI suggested adding meta tags, a theme color, and a favicon to the head to make it production-ready. It also provided instructions on how to use GitHub Pages or Netlify to host client-side only code for free.

**Prompt 4:** 
"just do as i say.make different files.its an order"

**What happened:** 
I forced the AI to break out of the single-file tool limitation. The AI corrected its approach and provided distinct blocks for `index.html`, `style.css`, `app.js`, `README.md`, and this `PROMPTS.md` file so they could be added individually to the GitHub repository. Code logic for search, local storage saving, and status updates works exactly as requested.
